1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,680
 If you get a pop-up that offers you 10% off,

2
00:00:02,680 --> 00:00:06,560
 and the button to close it says, "No thanks, it's even money,"

3
00:00:06,560 --> 00:00:08,120
 there's a name for that thing.

4
00:00:08,120 --> 00:00:09,880
 It's called a dark pattern.

5
00:00:09,880 --> 00:00:12,800
 A dark pattern is a design in an app or a website

6
00:00:12,800 --> 00:00:14,600
 that tricks you into doing something

7
00:00:14,600 --> 00:00:18,640
 that you don't mean to do, like buying something extra.

8
00:00:18,640 --> 00:00:21,800
 Now, a dark pattern is different from bad design.

9
00:00:21,800 --> 00:00:24,960
 Most of the time, dark patterns are intentional.

10
00:00:24,960 --> 00:00:27,000
 The dark pattern usually helps the business

11
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:28,800
 at the expense of the user.

12
00:00:28,800 --> 00:00:31,700
 There are a lot of dark patterns that people use to build stuff,

13
00:00:31,700 --> 00:00:33,520
 and I'll go over a lot of them in this video.

14
00:00:33,520 --> 00:00:35,680
 Most of all of the stuff I'm using in this video

15
00:00:35,680 --> 00:00:38,120
 come from Ari Brignall, who is a UX designer,

16
00:00:38,120 --> 00:00:41,920
 and he was the one that coined the term "dark patterns" in 2010.

17
00:00:41,920 --> 00:00:45,080
 I'll put the link to his website below.

18
00:00:45,080 --> 00:00:48,000
 The first one is the Confirmshaming.

19
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:51,420
 Confirmshaming is when the button to say "No"

20
00:00:51,420 --> 00:00:54,080
 is written to make the user feel bad.

21
00:00:54,080 --> 00:00:54,880
 Like, look at this.

22
00:00:54,880 --> 00:00:56,480
 No, I hate saving money.

23
00:00:56,480 --> 00:01:00,760
 The idea is that you'd rather click yes than say you ate saving money.

24
00:01:00,760 --> 00:01:03,080
 Now that pattern is hard to cancel.

25
00:01:03,080 --> 00:01:05,200
 People used to call it the Roach Motel.

26
00:01:05,200 --> 00:01:10,360
 The name comes from a cockroach trap where it's easy to get in, but it's hard to get out.

27
00:01:10,360 --> 00:01:14,880
 A lot of people can relate to this, where joining or subscribing is easy.

28
00:01:14,880 --> 00:01:17,680
 But leaving and/or unsubscribing is hard.

29
00:01:17,680 --> 00:01:20,720
 You join an online shop's delivery membership with one click.

30
00:01:20,720 --> 00:01:25,360
 But to cancel, you have to go through page after page after page of offers,

31
00:01:25,360 --> 00:01:31,080
 You know, like this, we also have another dark pattern called sneak into basket is

32
00:01:31,080 --> 00:01:34,200
 when something you didn't choose end up in your shopping cart.

33
00:01:34,200 --> 00:01:38,200
 So say you add a pair of shoes and now you get to check out.

34
00:01:38,200 --> 00:01:42,640
 There is also a cleaning spray for five euro that you never picked.

35
00:01:42,640 --> 00:01:46,920
 Not an interesting dark pattern is hidden costs where there are fees that only

36
00:01:46,920 --> 00:01:48,920
 show up at the last step of checkout.

37
00:01:48,920 --> 00:01:49,960
 This happens to me a lot.

38
00:01:49,960 --> 00:01:53,960
 You think that the total of your cart is 90 euro and now you are at checkout and

39
00:01:53,960 --> 00:01:55,520
 You're seeing 100 and something.

40
00:01:55,520 --> 00:01:58,080
 The shoe cost 40 euro on the product page.

41
00:01:58,080 --> 00:02:00,640
 And at the last page, you see that a service fee

42
00:02:00,640 --> 00:02:02,920
 has been hadded and is now 52 euros.

43
00:02:02,920 --> 00:02:05,280
 And by then you've typed in your card details,

44
00:02:05,280 --> 00:02:07,560
 so it just feels easier to just pay now.

45
00:02:07,560 --> 00:02:11,360
 Not a dark pattern in design is even subscription,

46
00:02:11,360 --> 00:02:14,640
 which is like a free trial that turns into a paid plan

47
00:02:14,640 --> 00:02:15,960
 without a clear warning.

48
00:02:15,960 --> 00:02:19,160
 So a shop gives you a free month of premium,

49
00:02:19,160 --> 00:02:20,280
 but it acts like a card first,

50
00:02:20,280 --> 00:02:22,760
 which is now is a dark pattern just so you know.

51
00:02:22,760 --> 00:02:24,620
 And when that free trial ends,

52
00:02:24,620 --> 00:02:26,060
 they just start charging you.

53
00:02:26,060 --> 00:02:27,140
 This has happened to me before.

54
00:02:27,140 --> 00:02:29,300
 They literally charged me for AES subscription.

55
00:02:29,300 --> 00:02:30,460
 There are many more dark patterns

56
00:02:30,460 --> 00:02:33,000
 that you will run into when you use apps or shops online.

57
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:34,180
 And again, keep in mind

58
00:02:34,180 --> 00:02:35,820
 that they were intentionally put in place.

59
00:02:35,820 --> 00:02:37,500
 It's not bad design, it's intentional.

60
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:39,180
 Another one is trick wording.

61
00:02:39,180 --> 00:02:40,700
 It's a question that is written

62
00:02:40,700 --> 00:02:43,700
 so that you click the opposite of what you're meant to click.

63
00:02:43,700 --> 00:02:45,740
 Something like, untick this box

64
00:02:45,740 --> 00:02:48,060
 if you don't want to stop receiving emails.

65
00:02:48,060 --> 00:02:50,180
 You would have to read these two or three times

66
00:02:50,180 --> 00:02:53,280
 to work out which one actually stops the email.

67
00:02:53,280 --> 00:02:56,500
 One other common dark pattern is disguised ads.

68
00:02:56,500 --> 00:02:59,700
 They are ads that are made to look like part of the page.

69
00:02:59,700 --> 00:03:02,460
 Say you search a shop for running shoes

70
00:03:02,460 --> 00:03:05,180
 and the first few results are paid ads

71
00:03:05,180 --> 00:03:07,100
 that look exactly like the normal result,

72
00:03:07,100 --> 00:03:08,260
 but they are paid ads.

73
00:03:08,260 --> 00:03:09,580
 We also have nagging.

74
00:03:09,580 --> 00:03:11,700
 Nagging is asking again and again and again

75
00:03:11,700 --> 00:03:13,300
 after the user has said no.

76
00:03:13,300 --> 00:03:14,940
 It's a type of dark pattern.

77
00:03:14,940 --> 00:03:17,460
 Say a shopping app asks you to turn on notification

78
00:03:17,460 --> 00:03:18,780
 and you're already saying no.

79
00:03:18,780 --> 00:03:20,300
 Next time it asks again.

80
00:03:20,300 --> 00:03:23,060
 And the only options are yes and not now.

81
00:03:23,060 --> 00:03:24,140
 This happens to me a lot.

82
00:03:24,140 --> 00:03:26,080
 I think Snapchat does this to me a lot.

83
00:03:26,080 --> 00:03:28,200
 I don't want my Snapchat notifications on,

84
00:03:28,200 --> 00:03:31,220
 but every time, every time it's asking me again and again,

85
00:03:31,220 --> 00:03:32,700
 I'm this close to deleting the app.

86
00:03:32,700 --> 00:03:34,180
 Another one is pre-selection.

87
00:03:34,180 --> 00:03:36,740
 Pre-selection is when the option that the company wants

88
00:03:36,740 --> 00:03:38,420
 is already ticked for the user.

89
00:03:38,420 --> 00:03:40,340
 So there is a box that checks out that say,

90
00:03:40,340 --> 00:03:44,020
 add gift wrapping for 2.99 euros, and it's already ticked.

91
00:03:44,020 --> 00:03:45,060
 If you don't read every line,

92
00:03:45,060 --> 00:03:47,380
 you pay for gift wrapping that you never asked for.

93
00:03:47,380 --> 00:03:49,860
 Another common dark pattern is fake urgency.

94
00:03:49,860 --> 00:03:52,440
 Fake urgency is a deadline that is not real.

95
00:03:52,440 --> 00:03:55,220
 Say a product page shows a countdown that says,

96
00:03:55,220 --> 00:03:57,180
 this deal ends in 10 minutes.

97
00:03:57,180 --> 00:03:59,500
 If you refresh that page again, it starts again

98
00:03:59,500 --> 00:04:00,500
 from 10 minutes.

99
00:04:00,500 --> 00:04:02,440
 This deal ends in 10 minutes.

100
00:04:02,440 --> 00:04:05,420
 It's there to rush you so you buy before you've had the time

101
00:04:05,420 --> 00:04:06,580
 to think about it.

102
00:04:06,580 --> 00:04:09,100
 Another one is fake scarcity.

103
00:04:09,100 --> 00:04:13,140
 This is done mostly by shopping website, shopping apps,

104
00:04:13,140 --> 00:04:15,500
 the website say that there's only a few left,

105
00:04:15,500 --> 00:04:16,860
 or that something is selling out.

106
00:04:16,860 --> 00:04:18,280
 when it's not true.

107
00:04:18,280 --> 00:04:21,580
 The PR issue will say, "Oh, only two left in stock."

108
00:04:21,580 --> 00:04:22,940
 And when you come back a week later,

109
00:04:22,940 --> 00:04:25,180
 it still says, "Only two left in stock."

110
00:04:25,180 --> 00:04:28,140
 It's there to make you buy now, buy now.

111
00:04:28,140 --> 00:04:30,220
 Why do companies use dark matter?

112
00:04:30,220 --> 00:04:33,580
 It's, a lot of times, it comes down to numbers.

113
00:04:33,580 --> 00:04:35,340
 Most product teams test their designs

114
00:04:35,340 --> 00:04:38,180
 with something called an A/B testing, A/B testing.

115
00:04:38,180 --> 00:04:39,780
 Half of the users see version A,

116
00:04:39,780 --> 00:04:41,540
 the other half see version B.

117
00:04:41,540 --> 00:04:44,340
 And whichever one gets more sales and sign up wins.

118
00:04:44,340 --> 00:04:47,540
 But an A/B test only measures the number you pick.

119
00:04:47,540 --> 00:04:50,020
 So you can't tell whether anyone was tricked.

120
00:04:50,020 --> 00:04:52,180
 A/B testing usually even doesn't work

121
00:04:52,180 --> 00:04:53,740
 for the benefit of the user.

122
00:04:53,740 --> 00:04:56,280
 It's just about what design is best.

123
00:04:56,280 --> 00:04:58,540
 It doesn't really judge which design is better

124
00:04:58,540 --> 00:04:59,840
 or worse for the user.

125
00:04:59,840 --> 00:05:01,860
 And I think this is how a lot of dark patterns

126
00:05:01,860 --> 00:05:03,100
 end up in apps.

127
00:05:03,100 --> 00:05:05,180
 A team is chasing a number, like,

128
00:05:05,180 --> 00:05:07,580
 "Oh, we want more sign-offs, or we want more sales."

129
00:05:07,580 --> 00:05:09,540
 So it's not really about what is best for the user.

130
00:05:09,540 --> 00:05:13,780
 It's about how much the team can get to their own goal.

131
00:05:13,780 --> 00:05:14,980
 How common are dark patterns?

132
00:05:14,980 --> 00:05:17,760
 In January, 2023, the European Commission

133
00:05:17,760 --> 00:05:21,680
 checked 399 online stores and 148 of them

134
00:05:21,680 --> 00:05:24,000
 had at least one of three dark patterns

135
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:25,800
 that the EU was looking for.

136
00:05:25,800 --> 00:05:29,400
 42 of those websites had fake countdowns, timers,

137
00:05:29,400 --> 00:05:32,700
 and 70 of them were hiding important information

138
00:05:32,700 --> 00:05:35,000
 or making important information hard to see.

139
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:38,200
 In July, 2024, consumer and privacy agencies

140
00:05:38,200 --> 00:05:42,800
 from about 26 countries, they looked at over 600 websites

141
00:05:42,800 --> 00:05:48,320
 self-subscriptions, and they found out that 76% of them use at least one possible dark pattern,

142
00:05:48,320 --> 00:05:53,760
 and nearly 67% of them use more than one dark pattern. So what's the law that protects users

143
00:05:53,760 --> 00:05:57,120
 against dark patterns? Are there even laws that protect users against dark patterns?

144
00:05:57,120 --> 00:06:01,440
 Depending on where you live, not a lawyer, but depending on where you live and everything that

145
00:06:01,440 --> 00:06:09,040
 I read and studied, in the EU, the Unfair Commercial Practices Directive from 2005 already banned some

146
00:06:09,040 --> 00:06:14,800
 of these tricks, like tricks like saying a deal is only available for a very limited time is banned

147
00:06:14,800 --> 00:06:20,000
 in the EU. So again protection from these dark patterns depends on where you live as a user.

148
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:27,200
 For your data, for example, GDPR says that a pre-ticked box does not count as consent on the

149
00:06:27,200 --> 00:06:33,600
 part of the user. So if an online shop charges you for an extra through a box that you have to

150
00:06:33,600 --> 00:06:38,720
 critique or pre-select for you, the EU consumer law says that you are entitled to get that money back.

151
00:06:39,040 --> 00:06:44,080
 In the US, I think the best known case is the Amazon Prime case, where the FTC said Amazon

152
00:06:44,080 --> 00:06:50,080
 signed people for Prime without their consent and made cancelling hard. So in September 2025,

153
00:06:50,080 --> 00:06:56,240
 Amazon actually did pay 2.5 billion dollars to settle this. And I read that the EU is working

154
00:06:56,240 --> 00:07:01,200
 on this new law called the Digital Fianness Act. The idea is that it's expected to deal with that

155
00:07:01,200 --> 00:07:05,760
 pattern more directly. It hasn't been published yet, but it's something that is under work.

156
00:07:05,760 --> 00:07:10,960
 So as a user, you just try to keep all of these patterns in mind as well to protect yourself.

157
00:07:10,960 --> 00:07:14,480
 And if you're a developer or a designer and maybe you work in a company and you actually

158
00:07:14,480 --> 00:07:18,800
 view the dark pattern, I think the way to counter it, if it's something you would want to counter,

159
00:07:18,800 --> 00:07:23,040
 is to be like, "Hey, we can get sued for this." Don't even try to say, "This is wrong," or anything

160
00:07:23,040 --> 00:07:26,640
 like that, because maybe you probably wouldn't be taking seriously. But just be like, "Hey, guys,

161
00:07:26,640 --> 00:07:30,960
 we could be sued for this." I think people take that more seriously. And try to build the honest

162
00:07:30,960 --> 00:07:36,320
 version well, I genuinely don't think users have to consistently, like users have been manipulated

163
00:07:36,320 --> 00:07:41,200
 in different places, we can make the world a better place, I don't know.

